This was one of the earliest racially integrated courses in the U.S. Army . Seventeen flight surgeons served with the Tuskegee Airmen from 1941 - 49 . At that time , the typical tour of duty for a U.S. Army flight surgeon was four years . Six of these physicians lived under field conditions during operations in North Africa , Sicily , and Italy . The chief flight surgeon to the Tuskegee Airmen was Dr. Vance H. Marchbanks , Jr. , MD , a childhood friend of Benjamin Davis . The accumulation of washed - out cadets at Tuskegee and the propensity of other commands to `` dump '' African - American personnel on the post exacerbated the difficulties of administering Tuskegee . A shortage of jobs for them made these enlisted men a drag on Tuskegee 's housing and culinary departments . Trained officers were also left idle , as the plan to shift African - American officers into command slots stalled , and white officers not only continued to hold command , but were joined by additional white officers assigned to the post .","entities":["Tuskegee","Vance H. Marchbanks, Jr.","Benjamin Davis","Sicily","Tuskegee Airmen","Italy","North Africa","U.S. Army"],"facts":["Tuskegee University -LOCATED_IN-> Tuskegee","budding flight program at Tuskegee -LOCATED_IN-> Tuskegee","Eleanor Roosevelt's inspection of the flight program -LOCATED_IN-> Tuskegee","Eleanor Roosevelt's half-hour flight with C. Alfred Anderson -LOCATED_IN-> Tuskegee","Tuskegee -LOCATED_IN-> Alabama"]}
