; for example California Vehicle Code section 22350 which states that `` No person shall drive a vehicle upon a highway at a speed greater than is reasonable ... and in no event at a speed which endangers the safety of persons or property '' . The basic speed law is almost always supplemented by specific maximum or minimum limits , but applies regardless . The reasonable speed may be different than the posted speed limit in conditions such as fog , heavy rain , ice , snow , gravel , sharp corners , blinding glare , darkness , crossing traffic , or when there is an obstructed view of orthogonal traffic -- such as by road curvature , parked cars , vegetation , or snow banks -- thus limiting the Assured Clear Distance Ahead ( ACDA ) . Basic speed laws are statutized reinforcements of the centuries - old common law negligence doctrine as specifically applied to vehicular speed .","entities":["reasonable speed","California Vehicle Code section 22350","Basic speed law","Specific maximum or minimum limits","Common law negligence doctrine","Vehicular speed","posted speed limit","Speed limit","Assured Clear Distance Ahead"],"facts":["posted speed limit -RELATED_TO-> roadways where virtually all motorists are in technical violation of the law","exceeding the posted speed limit -RELATED_TO-> posted speed limit","Basic speed law -RELATED_TO-> due process of law","California Department of Motor Vehicles -RELATED_TO-> Basic speed law","Low-speed electric vehicles -RELATED_TO-> posted speed limit"]}
