Variable speed limits ( edit ) Example variable speed limit sign in the United States Digital speed limit sign for variable speed limits In 1965 , the first known experiments with variable speed limit signs took place on a 30 km stretch of German motorway A8 between Munich and the border city of Salzburg , Austria . Mechanically variable message signs could display speeds of 60 , 80 and 100 km / h , `` danger zone '' or `` accident '' . Personnel monitored traffic using video technology , and manually controlled the signage . Beginning in the 1970s , additional advanced traffic control systems were put into service . Modern motorway control systems can work without human intervention using various types of sensors to measure traffic flow and weather conditions . In 2009 , 1,300 km ( 810 mi ) of German motorways were equipped with such systems . In the late 1960s , heavily traveled portions of the New Jersey Turnpike began using variable speed limit signs , in combination with variable message signs . Officials can adjust the speed limit according to weather , traffic conditions , and construction .","entities":["variable speed limit signs","variable message signs","A8","Salzburg","first known experiments with variable speed limit signs","use of variable speed limit signs on the New Jersey Turnpike","advanced traffic control systems","additional advanced traffic control systems put into service","modern motorway control systems","German motorways equipped with motorway control systems","variable speed limits","New Jersey Turnpike","Austria","Germany","Munich"],"facts":["West Autobahn -RELATED_TO-> Salzburg","variable speed limits -RELATED_TO-> United States","30 km long section of the A8 -PART_OF-> A8","First experiment -LOCATED_IN-> A8","Introduction of variable limits on Interstate 80 -RELATED_TO-> variable speed limits"]}
