072380 ° W ﻿ / 53.992020 ; - 1.072380 New Earswick is a model village and civil parish in the unitary authority of City of York in North Yorkshire , England , near the River Foss , north of York and south of Haxby . According to the 2001 census the parish had a population of 2,812 , reducing to 2,737 at the 2011 Census . Before 1996 it had been part of the Ryedale district . Contents ( hide ) 1 History 2 Governance 3 Community 4 Geography 5 Economy 6 Transport 7 Education 8 Religious sites 9 Sport 10 Gallery 11 References 12 External links History ( edit ) The village of New Earswick was built as a genuine mixed community . There was housing for both workers and managers , in a green setting with gardens for each home with its own 2 fruit trees . It was founded by the York philanthropist , Joseph Rowntree , who was quoted as saying , `` I do not want to establish communities bearing the stamp of charity but rather of rightly ordered and self governing communities '' .","entities":["City of York","North Yorkshire","River Foss","Haxby","Ryedale","Joseph Rowntree","New Earswick","York","England"],"facts":["Haxby to York City Centre route -RELATED_TO-> Haxby","Joseph Rowntree -RELATED_TO-> Earswick","Whitby harbour -LOCATED_IN-> North Yorkshire","Brimham Rocks -LOCATED_IN-> North Yorkshire","New Earswick -LOCATED_IN-> City of York"]}
[E8bb6ca] {"title":"New Earswick","text":"New Earswick
There is a library based in the Integrated Children 's Centre at the local primary school , a doctor 's surgery and a range of shops , including two general stores , a pet shop and Post Office . As of 2017 the Post Office is situated within the Folk Hall . The village has 36 allotments in two areas , Willow Bank and Sleeper Path . In the 1960s , the Folk Hall was a live music venue featuring the likes of Procol Harum and Pink Floyd . Geography ( edit ) The River Foss runs along the eastern boundary of New Earswick with the York to Scarborough Line running to the west . The Nestle factory and grounds mark the southern border with the northern border being the A1237 York Outer Ring Road . The village was built using bricks from the brickworks on the outskirts of the village . From 1950 the brickyard was developed into a nature reserve . The reserve is the home of New Earswick Angling Club , which was formed in 1948 .","entities":["River Foss","brickworks","nature reserve","New Earswick","Folk Hall","Integrated Children's Centre","local primary school","doctor's surgery","Willow Bank","Sleeper Path","York to Scarborough Line","Nestle factory and grounds","A1237 York Outer Ring Road","New Earswick Angling Club","library"],"facts":["development of the brickyard into a nature reserve -RELATED_TO-> nature reserve","New Earswick -LOCATED_IN-> City of York","closure of the brickyard -RELATED_TO-> brickworks","development of the brickyard into a nature reserve -RELATED_TO-> brickworks","New Earswick -LOCATED_IN-> Yorkshire and the Humber"]}
