[Eb954b0] {"title":"Fundamental rights in India","text":"Fundamental rights in India
The provisions relating to the right to property were changed a number of times . The Forty - Fourth Amendment of 1978 deleted the right to property from the list of fundamental rights A new provision , Article 300 - A , was added to the constitution , which provided that `` no person shall be deprived of his property save by authority of law '' . Thus if a legislature makes a law depriving a person of his property , there would be no obligation on the part of the State to pay anything as compensation . The aggrieved person shall have no right to move the court under Article 32 . Thus , the right to property is no longer a fundamental right , though it is still a constitutional right . If the government appears to have acted unfairly , the action can be challenged in a court of law by aggrieved citizens . The liberalisation of the economy and the government 's initiative to set up special economic zones has led to many protests by farmers and have led to calls for the reinstatement of the fundamental right to private property .","entities":["Legislature","Fundamental Rights","Constitution","Article 32","constitutional right","right to property","Forty-Fourth Amendment","Article 300-A","protests by farmers","calls for the reinstatement of the fundamental right to private property","India","State","Law","government","Special Economic Zones"],"facts":["Legislature -RELATED_TO-> upper house","Legislature -RELATED_TO-> JP courts","Legislature -RELATED_TO-> Laws","Democratic-Republicans -RELATED_TO-> Legislature","British king -RELATED_TO-> Legislature"]}
