In other words , the poet portrays kisses between a man and a woman as having the possibility of leading to sex , while in a heterosexual world kisses between a man and a man are portrayed as having no such possibility . Modern adaptations ( edit ) Books ( edit ) Though the surviving manuscript dates from the fourteenth century , the first published version of the poem did not appear until as late as 1839 , when Sir Frederic Madden of the British Museum recognized the poem as worth reading . Madden 's scholarly , Middle English edition of the poem was followed in 1898 by the first Modern English translation -- a prose version by literary scholar Jessie L. Weston . In 1925 , J.R.R. Tolkien and E.V. Gordon published a scholarly edition of the Middle English text of Sir Gawain and the Green Knight ; a revised edition of this text was prepared by Norman Davis and published in 1967 . The book , featuring a text in Middle English with extensive scholarly notes , is frequently confused with the translation into Modern English that Tolkien prepared , along with translations of Pearl and Sir Orfeo , late in his life .","entities":["British Museum","Pearl","E.V. Gordon","Sir Frederic Madden","Middle English edition of Sir Gawain and the Green Knight","Jessie L. Weston","Modern English translation of Sir Gawain and the Green Knight","Scholarly edition of the Middle English text of Sir Gawain and the Green Knight","Norman Davis","Revised edition of the Middle English text of Sir Gawain and the Green Knight","Tolkien's Modern English translation of Sir Gawain and the Green Knight","Translation of Pearl","Sir Orfeo","Translation of Sir Orfeo","Modern English"],"facts":["Room 52 -LOCATED_IN-> British Museum","Cyrus Cylinder -LOCATED_IN-> British Museum","Cyrus Cylinder -LOCATED_IN-> British Museum","British Museum -RELATED_TO-> Discovery of the Cyrus Cylinder","British Museum -LOCATED_IN-> London"]}
