Contents ( hide ) 1 After European colonization 2 Current issues 2.1 Species extinctions in the Eastern forests 2.2 Further reading 3 See also 4 References 5 External links After European colonization ( edit ) See also : Forests of the United States For the 300 years following the arrival of Europeans , land was cleared , mostly for agriculture , at a rate that matched that of population growth . For every person added to the population , one to two hectares of land was cultivated . This trend continued until the 1920s when the amount of crop land stabilized in spite of continued population growth . As abandoned farm land reverted to forest the amount of forest land increased reaching a peak in 1963 of 753,000,000 acres ( 3,050,000 km ) . After 1963 there was a gradual decrease through the next few decades which has been reversed with recovery and slight gains in the early 21st century . Gains in forest land have resulted from conversions from crop land and pastures at a higher rate than loss of forest to development .","entities":["United States","land","European colonization","Crop land","Abandoned farm land","Forest land","Forest land peak","Forest land decrease","Forest land recovery and slight gains","Pastures","1920s","Deforestation in the United States","Population growth","Development","Agriculture"],"facts":["United States -PART_OF-> Americas","United States -PART_OF-> Northern America","United States -PART_OF-> these ten countries","United States -PART_OF-> The Americas","United States -PART_OF-> Beekeeping by countries"]}
