uva , grape ) , also called the uveal layer , uveal coat , uveal tract , or vascular tunic , is the pigmented middle of the three concentric layers that make up an eye . The name is possibly a reference to its reddish - blue or almost black colour , wrinkled appearance and grape - like size and shape when stripped intact from a cadaveric eye . Its use as a technical term in anatomy and ophthalmology is relatively modern . Contents ( hide ) 1 Structure 1.1 Regions 2 Function 3 Pharmacology 4 Immunology 5 Clinical significance 6 References 7 External links Structure ( edit ) Regions ( edit ) The uvea is the vascular middle layer of the eye . It is traditionally divided into three areas , from front to back , the : Iris Ciliary body Choroid Function ( edit ) The prime functions of the uveal tract as a unit are : Nutrition and gas exchange : uveal vessels directly perfuse the ciliary body and iris , to support their metabolic needs , and indirectly supply diffusible nutrients to the outer retina , sclera , and lens , which lack any intrinsic blood supply .","entities":["eye","lens","outer retina","Sclera","Choroid","Ciliary body","uveal vessels","Nutrition and gas exchange","Uvea","Iris"],"facts":["cornea -LOCATED_IN-> eye","eye -RELATED_TO-> image-processing","eye -RELATED_TO-> detection of light direction","eye -RELATED_TO-> pinhole camera","eye -RELATED_TO-> eyepatches"]}
