After syngamy ( joining of the sperm and egg cell ) , the zygote develops into an embryo ( young sporophyte ) . More than one embryo is usually initiated in each gymnosperm seed . The mature seed comprises the embryo and the remains of the female gametophyte , which serves as a food supply , and the seed coat . Genetics ( edit ) The first published sequenced genome for any gymnosperm was the genome of Picea abies in 2013 . References ( edit ) ^ Jump up to : `` Gymnosperms on The Plant List '' . Theplantlist.org . Retrieved 2013 - 07 - 24 . ^ Jump up to : Raven , P.H. ( 2013 ) . Biology of Plants . New York : W.H. Freeman and Co . Jump up ^ Jeffrey D. Palmer ; Douglas E. Soltis ; Mark W. Chase ( 2004 ) . `` The plant tree of life : an overview and some points of view '' . American Journal of Botany. 91 ( 10 ) : 1437 -- 1445 .","entities":["New York","egg cell","Gymnosperm","syngamy","zygote","gymnosperm seed","female gametophyte","seed coat","food supply","Picea abies","genome of Picea abies","Gymnosperms on The Plant List","The Plant List","Raven, P.H.","W.H. Freeman and Co."],"facts":["New York -PART_OF-> US","Rochester -PART_OF-> New York","Brooklyn -PART_OF-> New York","Mount Pleasant -PART_OF-> New York","New York -PART_OF-> United States"]}
