As with the Roman temple portico front and its descendents in later classical architecture , trabeated features were often retained in parts of buildings as an aesthetic choice . The classical orders of Greek origin were in particular retained in buildings designed to impress , even though they usually had little or no structural role . Contents ( hide ) 1 Lintel beams 2 Engineering 3 See also 4 Notes 5 References Lintel beams ( edit ) Further information : Beam ( structure ) In architecture , a post-and - lintel or trabeated system refers to the use of horizontal beams or lintels which are borne up by columns or posts . The name is from the Latin trabs , beam ; influenced by trabeatus , clothed in the trabea , a ritual garment . A noteworthy example of a trabeated system is in Volubilis , from the Roman era , where one side of the Decumanus Maximus is lined with trabeated elements , while the opposite side of the roadway is designed in arched style . In India the style was used originally for wooden construction , but later the technique was adopted for stone structures for decorated load - bearing and purely ornamented non-structural purposes .","entities":["India","Latin","Post-and-lintel system","beam","column","post","Decumanus Maximus","classical orders","classical architecture","Roman temple portico front","trabs","trabeatus","trabea","ritual garment","wooden construction"],"facts":["India -PART_OF-> Beekeeping by countries","India -PART_OF-> British Empire","India -PART_OF-> these ten countries","India -PART_OF-> Gondwana","India -PART_OF-> National anthems of Asia"]}
