Large - enough quantities of ATP cause it to create a transmembrane proton gradient , this is used by fermenting bacteria that do not have an electron transport chain , but rather hydrolyze ATP to make a proton gradient , which they use to drive flagella and the transport of nutrients into the cell . In respiring bacteria under physiological conditions , ATP synthase , in general , runs in the opposite direction , creating ATP while using the proton motive force created by the electron transport chain as a source of energy . The overall process of creating energy in this fashion is termed oxidative phosphorylation . The same process takes place in the mitochondria , where ATP synthase is located in the inner mitochondrial membrane and the F - part projects into mitochondrial matrix . The consumption of ATP by ATP - synthase pumps proton cations into the matrix . Evolution ( edit ) The evolution of ATP synthase is thought to have been modular whereby two functionally independent subunits became associated and gained new functionality . This association appears to have occurred early in evolutionary history , because essentially the same structure and activity of ATP synthase enzymes are present in all kingdoms of life .","entities":["Evolution","Mitochondria","Nutrients","evolutionary history","proton gradient","ATP synthase","transmembrane proton gradient","fermenting bacteria","flagella","respiring bacteria","proton motive force","inner mitochondrial membrane","F-part","proton cations","two functionally independent subunits"],"facts":["Evolution -RELATED_TO-> Biology","Evolution -RELATED_TO-> Species","Evolution -RELATED_TO-> Darwin","Evolution -RELATED_TO-> embryological development","Evolution -RELATED_TO-> United States"]}
