For actively aiding the British army when it occupied Philadelphia , two residents of the city were tried for treason , convicted , and executed by returning Patriot forces . Slavery and black Loyalists ( edit ) Main article : Black Loyalist A Black Loyalist wood cutter at Shelburne , Nova Scotia in 1788 As a result of the looming crisis in 1775 the Royal Governor of Virginia , Lord Dunmore , issued a proclamation that promised freedom to servants and slaves who were able to bear arms and join his Loyalist Ethiopian Regiment . Many of the slaves in the South joined the Loyalists with intentions of gaining freedom and escaping the South . About 800 did so ; some helped rout the Virginia militia at the Battle of Kemp 's Landing and fought in the Battle of Great Bridge on the Elizabeth River , wearing the motto `` Liberty to Slaves '' , but this time they were defeated . The remains of their regiment were then involved in the evacuation of Norfolk , after which they served in the Chesapeake area . Eventually the camp that they had set up there suffered an outbreak of smallpox and other diseases .","entities":["Philadelphia","smallpox","British Army","Virginia","Loyalists","Patriot forces","proclamation","Ethiopian Regiment","Virginia militia","Battle of Kemp's Landing","Battle of Great Bridge","Elizabeth River","evacuation of Norfolk","Chesapeake area","outbreak of smallpox"],"facts":["Philadelphia -LOCATED_IN-> United States","39th and Chestnut Streets -LOCATED_IN-> Philadelphia","Lincoln Financial Field -LOCATED_IN-> Philadelphia","Eagles-Steelers meeting -LOCATED_IN-> Philadelphia","Philadelphia -LOCATED_IN-> Pennsylvania"]}

