Where Have All the Flowers Gone ? - Wikipedia Where Have All the Flowers Gone ? Jump to : navigation , search For the 2008 documentary , see Where Have All the Flowers Gone ? ( film ) . 1960 release on The Rainbow Quest LP , Folkways , FA 2454 , by Pete Seeger Columbia Hall of Fame 45 rpm single release as 13 - 33088 featuring the November 1962 version . August 1965 . `` Where Have All The Flowers Gone ? '' Song Language English Composer ( s ) Pete Seeger Lyricist ( s ) Pete Seeger and Joe Hickerson `` Where Have All the Flowers Gone ? '' is a modern folk - style song . The melody and the first three verses were written by Pete Seeger in 1955 and published in Sing Out ! magazine . Additional verses were added in May 1960 by Joe Hickerson , who turned it into a circular song . Its rhetorical `` where ? '' and meditation on death place the song in the ubi sunt tradition . In 2010 , the New Statesman listed it as one of the `` Top 20 Political Songs '' .","entities":["The Rainbow Quest","Folkways","ubi sunt","New Statesman","Top 20 Political Songs","Where Have All the Flowers Gone? (Columbia Hall of Fame single)","Columbia Hall of Fame","Joe Hickerson","Where Have All the Flowers Gone?","Sing Out!","Pete Seeger"],"facts":["Top 20 Political Songs: Where Have All the Flowers Gone -RELATED_TO-> New Statesman","Joe Hickerson -RELATED_TO-> Bloomington","New Statesman -RELATED_TO-> Top 20 Political Songs","Wilfrid Mellers -RELATED_TO-> New Statesman","Joe Hickerson -PARTICIPATED_IN-> 50th Annual Meeting"]}
[Ebae21e] {"title":"Where Have All the Flowers Gone?","text":"Where Have All the Flowers Gone?
Versions ( edit ) The Kingston Trio recorded the song in 1961 . Believing it to be a traditional song , they claimed authorship , although upon notice from Seeger they had their name removed and credited Seeger and Hickerson . Seeger acknowledged their success with this song . Their single , with `` O Ken Karanga '' as the A-side and the hit `` Where Have All The Flowers Gone ? '' the B - side , reached # 21 in the 1962 Billboard Hot 100 chart and # 4 on the Easy Listening chart . Peter , Paul and Mary included the song on their eponymous debut album ( which spent five weeks as the # 1 album in the country ) in 1962 . Marlene Dietrich performed the song in English , French , and German . The song was first performed in French ( as `` Qui peut dire où vont les fleurs ? '' ) by Marlene in 1962 at a UNICEF concert . She also recorded the song in English and in German , the latter titled `` Sag ' mir , wo die Blumen sind '' , with lyrics translated by Max Colpet .","entities":["French","English","Hickerson","German","Peter, Paul and Mary","The Kingston Trio","O Ken Karanga","Seeger","eponymous debut album","UNICEF concert","Max Colpet","traditional song","Where Have All the Flowers Gone?","Marlene Dietrich","UNICEF"],"facts":["French -RELATED_TO-> Hawaii","French -RELATED_TO-> Emperor's Court","French -RELATED_TO-> Wallonia","French -RELATED_TO-> Belgium","French -LOCATED_IN-> Africa"]}
[E357f43] {"title":"Neil Diamond","text":"Neil Diamond
It remains one of his personal all - time favorites , as it was autobiographical about his early years as a songwriter , even though he failed to realize it at the time : It was n't until years later , when I went into Freudian analysis , that I understood that it was me . It was an outgrowth of my despair . Diamond spent his early career as a songwriter in the Brill Building . His first success as a songwriter came in November 1965 , with `` Sunday and Me '' , a Top 20 hit for Jay and the Americans . Greater success as a writer followed with `` I 'm a Believer '' , `` A Little Bit Me , a Little Bit You '' , `` Look Out ( Here Comes Tomorrow ) '' , and `` Love to Love '' , all performed by the Monkees . Diamond wrote and recorded the songs for himself , but the cover versions were released before his own . The unintended , but happy , consequence was that Diamond began to gain fame not only as a singer and performer , but also as a songwriter .","entities":["The Monkees","Brill Building","Sunday and Me","Jay and the Americans","A Little Bit Me, a Little Bit You","Look Out (Here Comes Tomorrow)","Love to Love","Freudian analysis","I'm a Believer","Neil Diamond"],"facts":["The Monkees -RELATED_TO-> Should The Monkees be included in The Rock and Roll Hall of Fame?","The Monkees -RELATED_TO-> Hey, Hey Let Them In: 10 Reasons The Monkees Should Be in the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame","The Monkees -RELATED_TO-> NOW Can We Induct The Monkees Into The Rock And Roll Hall Of Fame?","The Monkees -RELATED_TO-> Rock and Roll Hall of Fame: The top 5 biggest omissions","The Monkees -RELATED_TO-> Rock and Roll Hall of Fame"]}

