In House of Bush , House of Saud , Craig Unger notes that at the time of Bush 's sale , Harken Energy `` was expected to run out of money in just three days '' ( p. 123 ) . In a last - ditch attempt to save the company , Harken was advised by the endowment fund of Harvard University to spin off two of its lower - performing divisions . `` According to a Harken memo , if the plan did not go through , the company had ' no other source of immediate financing . ' '' Bush had already taken out a $500,000 loan and sought Harken 's general counsel for advice . The reply was explicit : `` The act of trading , particularly if close in time to the receipt of the inside information , is strong evidence that the insider 's investment decision was based on the inside information ... the insider should be advised not to sell '' . This memo was turned over by Bush 's attorney the day after the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission ( SEC ) ruled that it would not charge Bush with insider trading .","entities":["U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission","Harvard University","Craig Unger","House of Bush, House of Saud","Harken Energy","endowment fund of Harvard University","Harken's general counsel","Harken memo","Bush's attorney","SEC ruling not to charge Bush with insider trading","insider trading","$500,000 loan","George W. Bush"],"facts":["U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission -CREATED-> Whistleblower Protection Act Information","Harvard University -RELATED_TO-> Guinea","Harvard University -RELATED_TO-> Liberia","Harvard University -RELATED_TO-> Sierra Leone","Harvard University -RELATED_TO-> Harvard update on West Africa Ebola outbreak: October 2014"]}
