Against the advice of his counsel , Bush repaid the loan by selling $848,000 worth of stock in Harken Energy . Harken reported significant financial losses within a year of this sale , triggering allegations of insider trading . On March 27 , 1992 , the Securities and Exchange Commission concluded that Bush had a `` preexisting plan '' to sell , that Bush had a `` relatively limited role in Harken management '' , and that it had not seen evidence of insider trading . The subsequent SEC investigation ended in 1992 with a memo stating `` it appears that Bush did not engage in illegal insider trading , '' but noted that the memo `` must in no way be construed as indicating that the party has been exonerated or that no action may ultimately result '' . Critics allege that this decision was strongly influenced by the makeup of the SEC at the time , which heavily favored Bush . The chairman at the time was Richard Breeden , a good friend of the Bush family 's who had been nominated to the SEC by President George H.W. Bush and who had been a lawyer in James Baker 's firm , Baker Botts .","entities":["Securities and Exchange Commission","Sale of Harken Energy stock","Allegations of insider trading","SEC investigation","Richard Breeden","Bush family","James Baker","SEC conclusion","SEC investigation memo","Baker Botts","Harken Energy","George W. Bush","George H.W. Bush"],"facts":["Baker Botts -RELATED_TO-> Texas Rangers baseball franchise","Baker Botts -RELATED_TO-> Saudi BinLaden family","Securities and Exchange Commission -PARTICIPATED_IN-> SEC decision not to charge Bush with insider trading","Wyeth Annual Report to Shareholders: 2005 -RELATED_TO-> Securities and Exchange Commission","Baker Botts -RELATED_TO-> James R. Bath"]}
