Its basis appears to be the old Roman Creed known also as the Old Roman Symbol . Because of the early origin of its original form , it does not address some Christological issues defined in the Nicene and other Christian Creeds . It thus says nothing explicitly about the divinity of either Jesus or the Holy Spirit . This makes it acceptable to many Arians and Unitarians . Nor does it address many other theological questions which became objects of dispute centuries later . The first mention of the expression `` Apostles ' Creed '' occurs in a letter of AD 390 from a synod in Milan and may have been associated with the belief , widely accepted in the 4th century , that , under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit , each of the Twelve Apostles contributed an article of a creed . Contents ( hide ) 1 Origin 2 Musical settings 3 Text in Latin 4 Text in Greek 5 English translations 5.1 Ecumenical version 5.2 Roman Catholic Church 5.2. 1 Articles 1 -- 12 5.2. 2 Articles 9 -- 10 5.3 Church of England 5.4 Lutheran Church 5.4.","entities":["Jesus","Arians","Milan","Apostles' Creed","Old Roman Creed","Christological issues","Nicene Creed","Holy Spirit","Unitarians","letter of AD 390","synod in Milan","Twelve Apostles","article of a creed"],"facts":["Jesus -RELATED_TO-> Kingdom of Heaven","Jesus -RELATED_TO-> Let My Love Open the Door","Jesus -RELATED_TO-> Septuagint","Jesus -RELATED_TO-> Messiah","Jesus -RELATED_TO-> James"]}
