One such factor is the ambient temperature . When conditions are warm , the onset and pace of rigor mortis are sped up by providing a conducive environment for the metabolic processes that cause decay . Low temperatures , however , slow them down . Therefore , for a person who dies outside in frozen conditions rigor mortis may last several days more than normal , so investigators may have to abandon it as a tool for determining time of death . See also ( edit ) Cadaveric spasm References ( edit ) ^ Jump up to : Saladin , K.S. 2010 . Anatomy & Physiology : 6th edition . McGraw - Hill . ^ Jump up to : Hall , John E. , and Arthur C. Guyton . Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology . Philadelphia , PA : Saunders / Elsevier , 2011 . MD Consult . Web . 26 Jan. 2015 . Jump up ^ Fremery , Donald ( 3 February 1959 ) . `` Biochemistry of Chicken Muscle as Related to Rigor Mortis and Tenderization '' . Journal of Food Sciences . 25 ( 1 ) : 73 -- 87 .","entities":["McGraw-Hill","Philadelphia, PA","Saunders / Elsevier","John E. Hall","Cadaveric spasm","Saladin, K.S.","Anatomy & Physiology","Arthur C. Guyton","Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","MD Consult","Donald Fremery","Biochemistry of Chicken Muscle as Related to Rigor Mortis and Tenderization","Journal of Food Sciences","Rigor mortis"],"facts":["McGraw-Hill -LOCATED_IN-> New York","McGraw-Hill -RELATED_TO-> Anatomy and Physiology: The Unity of Form and Function","McGraw-Hill -RELATED_TO-> Psychometric theory","McGraw-Hill -RELATED_TO-> McGraw-Hill Encyclopedia of Science & Technology","Principles of neural science -RELATED_TO-> McGraw-Hill"]}
