The calcium ions trigger powerful muscle contraction aided by ATP molecules . To prevent cold shortening , a process known as electrical stimulation is carried out , especially in beef carcasses , immediately after slaughter and skinning . In this process , the carcass is stimulated with alternating current , causing it to contract and relax , which depletes the ATP reserve from the carcass and prevents cold shortening . Application in forensic pathology ( edit ) The degree of rigor mortis may be used in forensic pathology , to determine the approximate time of death . A dead body holds its position as rigor mortis sets in . If the body is moved after death , but before rigor mortis begins , forensic techniques such as livor mortis can be applied . If the position in which a body is found does not match the location where it is found ( for example , if it is flat on its back with one arm sticking straight up ) , that could mean someone moved it . Several factors also affect the progression of rigor mortis , and investigators take these into account when estimating the time of death . One such factor is the ambient temperature .","entities":["investigators","muscle contraction","cold shortening","calcium ions","ATP molecules","electrical stimulation","beef carcasses","slaughter","skinning","alternating current","carcass","ATP reserve","forensic pathology","time of death","dead body"],"facts":["investigators -RELATED_TO-> target","investigators -RELATED_TO-> controlled range","investigators -RELATED_TO-> communications channels","muscle contraction -RELATED_TO-> calcium","muscle contraction -RELATED_TO-> myosin heads"]}
