[Ea09e57] {"title":"Pencil","text":"Pencil
Consequently , it was called plumbago ( Latin for `` lead ore '' ) . Because the pencil core is still referred to as `` lead '' , or a `` lead '' , many people have the misconception that the graphite in the pencil is lead , and the black core of pencils is still referred to as lead , even though it never contained the element lead . The words for pencil in German ( bleistift ) , Irish ( peann luaidhe ) , Arabic ( قلم رصاص qalam raṣāṣ ) , and some other languages literally mean lead pen . The value of graphite would soon be realised to be enormous , mainly because it could be used to line the moulds for cannonballs ; the mines were taken over by the Crown and were guarded . When sufficient stores of graphite had been accumulated , the mines were flooded to prevent theft until more was required . The usefulness of graphite for pencils was discovered as well , but graphite for pencils had to be smuggled . The news of the usefulness of these early pencils spread far and wide , attracting the attention of artists all over the known world . Because graphite is soft , it requires some form of encasement .","entities":["Irish","Crown","German","Arabic","pencil core","Pencil","lead","graphite","mines","cannonballs","lead ore"],"facts":["Hawaii -RELATED_TO-> Irish","Father Thomas Byles -RELATED_TO-> Irish","Richard Harris -RELATED_TO-> Irish","Cá bhfuil siad uainn, scoth na mbláth? -RELATED_TO-> Irish","Crown -RELATED_TO-> East India Company"]}
