E.g. , An anagram for `` debit card '' is `` bad credit '' . As you can see , both phrases use the same letters . By mixing the letters a bit of humor is created . Asyndeton When sentences do not use conjunctions ( e.g. , and , or , nor ) to separate clauses , but run clauses into one another , usually marking the separation of clauses with punctuation . An example is when John F. Kennedy said on January 20 , 1961 `` ... that we shall pay any price , bear any burden , meet any hardship , support any friend , oppose any foe to assure the survival and the success of liberty . '' Bathos An abrupt transition in style from the exalted to the commonplace , producing a ludicrous effect . While often unintended , bathos may be used deliberately to produce a humorous effect . : The ballerina rose gracefully en pointe and extended one slender leg behind her , like a dog at a fire hydrant . ( Jennifer Hart , Arlington ) Caesura A break , especially a sense pause , usually near the middle of a verse , and marked in scansion by a double vertical line .","entities":["John F. Kennedy","caesura","Arlington","Anagram","debit card","bad credit","Asyndeton","Bathos","Jennifer Hart","humor"],"facts":["John F. Kennedy -RELATED_TO-> Steven Keaton","John F. Kennedy -RELATED_TO-> Elyse Keaton","John F. Kennedy -RELATED_TO-> State Arrival Ceremony","John F. Kennedy -RELATED_TO-> images of the presidency","John F. Kennedy -LOCATED_IN-> Massachusetts"]}
