[E613455] {"title":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","text":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct
However , the Model Rules have been adopted , in whole or in part , and sometimes in variation , as the rules of professional conduct for attorneys in 49 states , the District of Columbia , and four of the five inhabited U.S. territories . The rules of professional conduct or professional responsibility adopted in a particular state are legally enforceable against the lawyers of that state as well as any lawyer practicing there on a pro hac vice basis . On December 17 , 2008 , New York announced that it would finally abandon the old ABA Model Code of Professional Responsibility ( the last state to do so ) and adopt a heavily modified version of the ABA Model Rules , effective April 1 , 2009 . New York 's version of the Model Rules was created by adjusting the standard Model Rules to reflect indigenous New York rules that had been incorporated over the years into its version of the Model Code .","entities":["New York","49 states","Model Rules of Professional Conduct","ABA Model Code of Professional Responsibility","four of the five inhabited U.S. territories","New York's version of the Model Rules","indigenous New York rules","attorneys","pro hac vice","District of Columbia","American Bar Association"],"facts":["New York -PART_OF-> US","Rochester -PART_OF-> New York","Brooklyn -PART_OF-> New York","Mount Pleasant -PART_OF-> New York","New York -PART_OF-> United States"]}
[E928efc] {"title":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","text":"American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct
Even though New York did not adopt the Model Rules verbatim , the advantage of adopting its overall structure is that it simplifies the professional responsibility training of New York lawyers , and makes it easier for out - of - state lawyers to conform their conduct to New York rules by simply comparing their home state 's version of the Model Rules to New York 's version . In June 2009 , the Supreme Court of Maine approved the adoption of the Model Rules in that state , effective August 1 , 2009 . Among the portions of the Model Rules that the Maine Rules of Professional Conduct do not include is Model Rule 1.8 ( j ) ( 2002 ) categorically prohibiting sexual relations between lawyer and client . In March 2015 , the Supreme Court of the Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands adopted its current Rules of Attorney Discipline and Procedure , which incorporates the ABA Model Rules by reference and expands upon them by adding another 22 local rules of professional conduct . California ( edit ) California still has its own set of unique rules governing professional attorney conduct .","entities":["New York","California","Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands","New York's version of the Model Rules","Supreme Court of Maine","Approval of adoption of the Model Rules in Maine","Maine Rules of Professional Conduct","Model Rule 1.8(j)","Supreme Court of the Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands","Adoption of the Rules of Attorney Discipline and Procedure","Rules of Attorney Discipline and Procedure","local rules of professional conduct","California's rules governing professional attorney conduct","American Bar Association Model Rules of Professional Conduct","Maine"],"facts":["New York -PART_OF-> US","Rochester -PART_OF-> New York","Brooklyn -PART_OF-> New York","Mount Pleasant -PART_OF-> New York","New York -PART_OF-> United States"]}
