The optic chiasm is located at the bottom of the brain immediately below the hypothalamus . The optic chiasm is found in all vertebrates , although in cyclostomes ( lampreys and hagfishes ) it is located within the brain . Contents ( hide ) 1 Structure 2 Other animals 3 Additional images 4 History 5 See also 6 References 7 External links Structure ( edit ) The optic nerve fibres on the nasal sides of each retina cross over ( decussate ) to the opposite side of the brain via the optic nerve at the optic chiasm ( decussation of medial fibers ) . The temporal hemiretina , on the other hand , stays on the same side . The inferonasal retina are related to anterior portion of the optic chiasm whereas superonasal retinal fibers are related to the posterior portion of the optic chiasm . The crossing over of optic nerve fibres at the optic chiasm allows the visual cortex to receive the same hemispheric visual field from both eyes . Superimposing and processing these monocular visual signals allow the visual cortex to generate binocular and stereoscopic vision .","entities":["eyes","optic nerve","Retina","vertebrates","hypothalamus","Brain","optic chiasm","cyclostomes","lampreys","hagfishes","optic nerve fibres","temporal hemiretina","inferonasal retina","anterior portion of the optic chiasm","superonasal retinal fibers"],"facts":["protostomes -RELATED_TO-> eyes","microvilli -RELATED_TO-> eyes","deuterostomes -RELATED_TO-> eyes","microvilliar cells -RELATED_TO-> eyes","eyes -LOCATED_IN-> head"]}
